---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import SolarCompassWidget from '../../components/SolarCompassWidget.astro';
import RelatedTools from '../../components/RelatedTools.astro';
import { breadcrumbs, webApplication } from '../../lib/jsonld';

const canonicalPath = '/dates/shadow/';

const jsonLd = [
  webApplication({
    name: 'Shadow Length Calculator & Solar Compass',
    description:
      'See how long a shadow is at any time, date and latitude, and drag the sun across the sky to watch it turn.',
    path: canonicalPath,
  }),
  breadcrumbs([
    { name: 'Home', path: '/' },
    { name: 'Dates & Time', path: '/dates/' },
    { name: 'Shadow Length', path: canonicalPath },
  ]),
];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Shadow Length Calculator · Solar Compass & Sundial"
  description="Find out how long a shadow is right now, or pick any date, time and latitude. Drag the sun, scrub the year and watch the shadow turn. Free and shareable."
  canonicalPath={canonicalPath}
  jsonLd={jsonLd}
>
  <nav class="text-sm text-zinc-500 dark:text-zinc-400" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <a href="/" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-zinc-100">Home</a>
    <span class="mx-2">/</span>
    <a href="/dates/" class="hover:text-zinc-900 dark:hover:text-zinc-100">Dates &amp; Time</a>
    <span class="mx-2">/</span>
    <span>Shadow Length</span>
  </nav>

  <h1 class="mt-4 text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight sm:text-4xl">
    Shadow length &amp; solar compass
  </h1>
  <p class="mt-3 max-w-xl text-zinc-600 dark:text-zinc-400">
    Drag the sun across the dial to see how long a shadow gets and which way it
    points. Scrub the outer ring to travel through the year. Everything is
    computed in your browser, and the whole state lives in the URL.
  </p>

  <div class="mt-8">
    <SolarCompassWidget />
  </div>

  <section class="mt-12 space-y-3 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">How it works</h2>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      The dial runs on the NOAA solar position equations. For the chosen day the
      tool derives the solar declination and the equation of time, then turns the
      clock time and the longitude into an hour angle. Latitude, declination and
      hour angle give the sun's height above the horizon and its compass
      bearing. A shadow cast by an object of height <em>h</em> is then
      <em>h</em> / tan(elevation) long and points exactly away from the sun.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      The centre of the dial is straight up and the rim is the horizon, so the
      sun's distance from the centre is its angular distance from the zenith. Its
      angle around the dial is the compass bearing, with north at the top. The
      grey line runs from the centre in the opposite direction: that is where the
      shadow falls.
    </p>
    <p class="text-sm leading-relaxed">
      The outer ring has one tick per day of the year, and each tick's length is
      that day's daylight. Drag it and the seasons unfold: long ticks in summer,
      short ones in winter, and a gap at high latitudes during the polar night.
      A faint figure eight sits inside the dial. It is the analemma, the place
      where the sun hangs at the same clock time on every day of the year; the
      width comes from the equation of time, which makes solar noon drift by up
      to a quarter of an hour either side of the clock.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 space-y-3 text-zinc-700 dark:text-zinc-300">
    <h2 class="text-xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Reading the numbers</h2>
    <ul class="list-disc space-y-2 pl-5 text-sm leading-relaxed">
      <li>
        <strong>Sun height</strong> is the elevation above the horizon. At 45°
        the shadow is exactly as long as the object is tall.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shadow ratio</strong> is the one photographers care about: 2.0×
        means the shadow is twice the height of the object.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Shadow points</strong> is a compass bearing. In the northern
        hemisphere the shadow points north at solar noon; in the southern
        hemisphere it points south.
      </li>
      <li>
        Times are local clock times for the chosen time zone, so they already
        include daylight saving. Daylight itself uses the official 90.833°
        horizon, which accounts for refraction and the size of the sun's disc.
      </li>
      <li>
        Drag the person up and down in the little scene to change the object
        height. The dial itself is keyboard friendly too: focus it and use the
        arrow keys.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="mt-10 text-sm">
    <a
      href="/dates/sunrise-sunset/"
      class="text-zinc-600 underline hover:text-zinc-900 dark:text-zinc-400 dark:hover:text-zinc-100"
    >
      Need the exact sunrise and sunset times? Use the sunrise calculator →
    </a>
  </section>

  <RelatedTools category="dates" current={canonicalPath} />
</BaseLayout>
